---
// INVEST — rebuilt 2026-09-07 as a pre-seed investor page.
//
// THE ROUND SIZE IS NOW DERIVED, NOT GUESSED. An earlier draft said "$2M–$5M" on the strength of
// a single band in one article. That was too vague to survive a partner meeting: any investor
// prices the compute line in their head in about ten seconds, and a range that wide reads as a
// founder who has not done it. The figure below is built bottom-up from published 2026 rates and
// benchmarked against pre-seed market data. Both are shown on the page, because showing the
// arithmetic is itself the credential.
//
//   MARKET (H1 2026): broad pre-seed median ~$1.2M, range $500K–$2.5M. Deep tech and AI
//   infrastructure raise $2M–$5M because of compute. AI seed median $4.6M, a 1.3x premium.
//
//   BOTTOM-UP: team $650K + compute $250K + one dataset partnership $150K + structure and
//   filings $100K + operating $200K + buffer $150K = $1.5M over 18 months.
//
// RESIZED 2026-09-15, FROM $4.5M/24mo TO $1.5M/18mo, and the reasoning is the substance of the
// page rather than a detail of it.
//
//   The $4.5M version was costed honestly and was still the wrong ask. It bought frontier-scale
//   validation — 800 accelerators for a bounded campaign — which is a seed-stage result being
//   funded at pre-seed on a pre-seed track record: no revenue, no customers, one person, CIFAR.
//   It also sat at the top of the deep tech band, which invites the reader to price the company
//   against companies with traction and find it short.
//
//   $1.5M is sized to THE NEXT RESULT rather than to the whole plan. It buys the second and third
//   person, a bounded scaling study on 32–64 GPUs run twice, and one regulated dataset. Frontier
//   scale, multiple sectors and a commercial team are a seed round, and they should be priced
//   against evidence that exists by then rather than against this page.
//
//   The tactical consequence matters too. This company is applying to fellowships, grants and
//   accelerators whose cheques run $50K–$1M. A $4.5M banner reads as a miscalibrated
//   self-assessment to every one of them. $1.5M with named milestones reads as a founder who
//   knows what stage they are at, and it leaves a natural entry point at every cheque size.
//
// HOW THIS PAGE IS ORDERED, and why it is not the order of the rest of the site. Investor
// research is consistent on three things: the insight is the strongest asset and goes first; the
// silent question behind every "why now" is "if this is a good idea, why has nobody built it";
// and money is committed against de-risking, not against science. So: insight, then why the
// window is open and dated, then what is already de-risked, then what this round de-risks next,
// then what remains true and unflattering. The risk register is on the page deliberately — deep
// tech investors read its absence as naivety, and its presence is the cheapest credibility on
// offer.
//
// THE MOAT ARGUMENT IS "UNCOMPRESSIBLE TIME", not secrecy. The mechanism is published, so the
// defensibility cannot rest on nobody knowing it. It rests on what capital cannot compress:
// regulatory acceptance, calibration history, and an adversarial record. That is the strongest
// available position for a published architecture and it is stated as such rather than hedged.
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PageBanner from '../components/PageBanner.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';

const raise = {
	stage: 'Pre-seed',
	size: '$1.5M',
	runway: '18 months',
};

// Published H1 2026 benchmarks. On the page because pricing yourself against the market, out loud,
// is a stronger signal than any adjective — and because an investor is holding these numbers
// anyway.
const benchmarks = [
	{ k: 'Broad pre-seed median', v: '$1.2M', note: 'All sectors, H1 2026. Range $500K–$2.5M.' },
	{ k: 'Deep tech and AI infrastructure', v: '$2M–$5M', note: 'Raised above the median, specifically to cover compute.' },
	{ k: 'AI seed median', v: '$4.6M', note: 'A 1.3x premium on the broader seed market.' },
	{ k: 'This round', v: '$1.5M', note: 'Deliberately below the deep tech band. The reason is the paragraph under this table.' },
];

// (6) THE ONE-SCREEN SUMMARY. This page is ~2,600 words inside a 45,000-word site, and a partner
// meeting runs on something a single person can forward. Six lines, ~210 words, every figure
// repeated verbatim from further down the page so the summary can never drift from the argument
// it summarises. It sits above everything because the reader who needs it most is the one who
// will not scroll.
const brief = [
	{ k: 'The insight', v: 'Every interpretability and monitoring tool estimates a model from outside it, and an estimate is an opinion. ORMAS bounds each node’s gradient chain to four operations, so attribution stops being an estimate and becomes a measurement taken during training. If that is right, every regulated training run eventually has to be built this way, and the layer the market is currently funding is the wrong layer.' },
	{ k: 'What exists', v: 'The question in 2023; two years of research in 2024–25, the first of them a failure against its own destruction suite; a first working version on 11 July 2026, and the complete system on 1 August 2026. 383 controlled experiments across four architecture families, 67 archived runs reproducible from seed, 16,316 lines of instrumentation. +70.3 pp recovery from catastrophic structural collapse. One adverse result, disclosed. Not yet independently verified.' },
	{ k: 'Why now', v: 'The obligations arrive on legislated dates, not forecast ones: SR 26-2 already in force, EU AI Act Annex III from 2 December 2027, Annex I from 2 August 2028. An architecture cannot be retrofitted into a model that has already been trained.' },
	{ k: 'The moat', v: 'The mechanism publishes on release, so defensibility never rested on secrecy. It rests on what capital cannot compress: regulatory acceptance, calibration history, and an adversarial record.' },
	{ k: 'The ask', v: '$1.5M pre-seed, 18 months, six milestones with acceptance criteria. Team $650K · compute $250K · one dataset partnership $150K · structure and filings $100K · operating $200K · buffer $150K. Sized to the next result rather than to the whole plan, and deliberately below the deep tech band.' },
	{ k: 'The risk', v: 'No revenue, no customers, zero customer conversations. One person. Every result on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100. The whole remaining programme comes down to four specified measurements, which is the fastest way to find out if we are wrong.' },
];

/* ── THE MARKET, COUNTED UPWARD ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   Built from the institutions that qualify rather than divided down from an
   analyst's total addressable figure. A top-down number is unfalsifiable and every
   investor knows it; a bottom-up one can be argued with, which is the point. The
   segment counts are our own and are flagged as such on the page — they are the
   figure here most likely to be wrong. */
const segments = [
	{ k: 'Academic medical centres', v: '~180', note: 'With an internal AI group and a regulated pathway.' },
	{ k: 'Funds and asset managers', v: '~400', note: 'With in-house machine learning.' },
	{ k: 'Insurers and credit-risk teams', v: '~270', note: 'Under supervisory reporting.' },
	{ k: 'Labs running training at cost', v: '~150', note: 'Where a failed run is a budget line, not an inconvenience.' },
];

// The four people around one workflow. A sale needs all four, and each fails it
// differently — which is why they are on the page as four separate positions
// rather than one composite "buyer".
const roles = [
	{
		title: 'The economic buyer — Head of Model Risk',
		body: 'Owns the budget and the remediation timetable. Wants to clear validation first time. Fears being the name on a model an examiner later reopens.',
	},
	{
		title: 'The technical user — machine-learning platform lead',
		body: 'Runs the training and owns the serving path. Wants to stop losing six-figure runs to failures found thousands of steps late. Fears anything that changes inference cost.',
	},
	{
		title: 'The risk owner — Chief Risk Officer',
		body: 'Signs, and carries it to the board. Wants a defensible position rather than a better number. Fears the sentence “the model changed and we cannot say how” appearing in a supervisory letter.',
	},
	{
		title: 'The gatekeeper — independent validation, then the examiner',
		body: 'Wants something verifiable rather than asserted. Kills deals by rejecting a method it cannot reproduce.',
	},
];

/* WHAT A BUYER ALREADY OWNS. Five named vendors, and the row that actually matters
   is the second to last: the incumbent is not a competitor, it is retraining from
   scratch. Listing the vendors as complements rather than casualties is also the
   honest reading — they share the buyer and the budget line, and none of them
   claims to answer what training did. */
const alternatives = [
	{ name: 'ModelOp', sells: 'Governance control tower', record: false, attr: false, repair: false },
	{ name: 'Credo AI', sells: 'Policy and compliance documentation', record: false, attr: false, repair: false },
	{ name: 'Fiddler AI', sells: 'Post-hoc explainability', record: false, attr: 'partial', repair: false },
	{ name: 'Arthur AI', sells: 'Production drift monitoring', record: false, attr: false, repair: false },
	{ name: 'Robust Intelligence (Cisco)', sells: 'Pre-deployment stress testing', record: false, attr: false, repair: false },
	{ name: 'Retraining from scratch', sells: 'The status quo remedy', record: false, attr: false, repair: false, incumbent: true },
	{ name: 'ORMAS', sells: 'The account, produced as it trains', record: true, attr: true, repair: true, ours: true },
];

// Three years, every cell an assumption and labelled as one. The ratio flatters
// because the inputs are unproven, and the page says so directly underneath.
const plan = [
	{ k: 'Paying institutions', y1: '0', y2: '4', y3: '20', note: 'Land at one model, then expand' },
	{ k: 'ARR', y1: '$0', y2: '$1.0M', y3: '$5.0M', note: '$250K ACV, held flat' },
	{ k: 'Gross margin', y1: '—', y2: '~92%', y3: '~92%', note: 'On-premise; no hosting cost' },
	{ k: 'CAC, fully loaded', y1: '—', y2: '~$85K', y3: '~$85K', note: 'Founder and commercial time, pilot engineering' },
	{ k: 'LTV, four-year, margin-adjusted', y1: '—', y2: '$920K', y3: '$920K', note: 'Four-year life, no expansion assumed' },
	{ k: 'LTV:CAC', y1: '—', y2: '~10.8:1', y3: '~10.8:1', note: 'Benchmark: 3:1 healthy, 5:1 strong', lead: true },
	{ k: 'Net burn', y1: '$820K', y2: '$680K', y3: 'Near breakeven', note: 'Compute front-loaded to year one' },
];

/* WHERE EVERY NUMBER COMES FROM. Three of the seven rows are unvalidated, and those
   three are what this round exists to validate. A provenance table is the cheapest
   credibility on an investor page precisely because it is the one thing a founder
   with weak numbers cannot publish. */
const provenance = [
	{ figure: '80.3% against 10.0%, +70.3 pp', source: '383 controlled experiments, reproducible from seed', status: 'Measured, replayable' },
	{ figure: '$8.3B market, 9.7% CAGR', source: 'Third-party analyst, AI model-risk management, 2026', status: 'Third-party' },
	{ figure: '~1,000 institutions', source: 'Our own bottom-up count across four segments', status: 'Estimate, unvalidated', weak: true },
	{ figure: '$250K ACV, $150K–$500K band', source: 'Benchmarked against model-risk tooling', status: 'No pricing conversations', weak: true },
	{ figure: 'CAC $85K, LTV:CAC 10.8:1', source: 'Modelled on a six-to-eighteen-month enterprise cycle', status: 'Assumption, no data', weak: true },
	{ figure: '$15M cap, 10% dilution', source: '2026 AI pre-seed band; $15M is the norm at this raise size', status: 'Market benchmark' },
	{ figure: 'Competitor pricing ~$50K', source: 'Published enterprise quote floors', status: 'Third-party' },
];

const stats = [
	{ figure: '+70.3', unit: 'pp', label: 'Recovery from catastrophic structural collapse, where a parameter-matched baseline is permanently dead' },
	{ figure: '383', unit: '', label: 'Controlled experiments across four architecture families, every run reproducible from seed' },
	{ figure: '3', unit: 'losses', label: 'Published runs where ORMAS finishes behind the baseline, named next to the wins' },
	{ figure: '1', unit: 'person', label: 'Who built all of it. The second is the most consequential thing this round buys' },
];

// The regulatory calendar. Every date here is legislated rather than forecast, which is the whole
// argument of the section — the demand event has a date on it and the architecture has to exist
// before it, not after.
const CH = { w: 780, h: 0, rowH: 48, top: 56, labelW: 274, plotR: 752 };
const events = [
	{ date: '2026-04-17', label: 'SR 26-2 supersedes SR 11-7', who: 'Fed · FDIC · OCC', past: true },
	{ date: '2026-07-27', label: 'EU AI Act Omnibus in force', who: 'European Commission', past: true },
	{ date: '2027-12-02', label: 'Annex III high-risk obligations apply', who: 'EU AI Act', past: false },
	{ date: '2028-08-02', label: 'Annex I obligations apply', who: 'EU AI Act', past: false },
	{ date: '2029-03-01', label: 'European Health Data Space, first phase', who: 'EHDS', past: false },
	{ date: '2031-03-01', label: 'European Health Data Space, full scope', who: 'EHDS', past: false },
];
CH.h = CH.top + events.length * CH.rowH + 24;
const T0 = Date.parse('2026-01-01');
const T1 = Date.parse('2032-01-01');
const x = (d: string) => CH.labelW + ((Date.parse(d) - T0) / (T1 - T0)) * (CH.plotR - CH.labelW);
const years = [2026, 2027, 2028, 2029, 2030, 2031];
const today = '2026-09-07';

const uses = [
	{
		n: '01',
		title: 'The second and third person',
		amount: '~$650K',
		pct: 43,
		unit: 'A co-founder and one research engineer, 18 months, fully loaded',
		body: 'The company is one person. That is the largest single risk on this page and it is the first thing the round removes. A co-founder to carry the commercial and institutional side, which is the half this founder has not done, and one research engineer able to extend the architecture independently rather than execute instructions. Two hires rather than five, because three people who can each work unsupervised is a company, and five people managed by a first-time manager during a scaling study is a different and worse problem.',
		milestone: 'Two people, beyond the founder, each able to extend the architecture or close an institutional conversation without supervision.',
		removes: 'Key-person risk',
	},
	{
		n: '02',
		title: 'Bounded compute',
		amount: '~$250K',
		pct: 17,
		unit: '32–64 accelerators, a 3–4 week campaign, run twice',
		body: 'Every result to date runs on CIFAR, because that is what one person could afford. This does not buy frontier scale and the page will not pretend otherwise. It buys the answer to whether bounded attribution survives well beyond the regime it was demonstrated in, on real model sizes and a transformer backbone, with the adversarial suite the current architecture was hardened against. Two campaigns rather than one: the first returns a result, the second answers what the first raises. Reserved and released, never standing, because idle accelerators are the most common way a round this size disappears with nothing attached to it.',
		milestone: 'Bounded attribution demonstrated on a transformer backbone at a model size well beyond CIFAR, every run reproducible from seed, and the overhead figure measured rather than estimated.',
		removes: 'Scale risk',
	},
	{
		n: '03',
		title: 'One dataset partnership',
		amount: '~$150K',
		pct: 10,
		unit: 'A single non-exclusive regulated corpus, plus the legal work around it',
		body: 'No clinical, biological, financial or defence data has ever touched this system, and that is the binding constraint on the entire thesis. One partnership, not three. A property demonstrated on one regulated corpus is not yet proof it generalises across sectors, and claiming otherwise would be the same overreach this page exists to avoid. What it does establish is that a custodian released data to this architecture, which is the single fact no amount of further CIFAR work can produce.',
		milestone: 'One signed partnership with a custodian holding data under a real obligation, and the property demonstrated on that data.',
		removes: 'Domain risk',
	},
	{
		n: '04',
		title: 'Structure and filings',
		amount: '~$100K',
		pct: 7,
		unit: 'Delaware conversion, EU Article 27 representation, priority patent filings',
		body: 'Gates rather than ornaments. The Delaware conversion has to complete before a US institutional round can close into the company at all. An EU Article 27 representative is a legal requirement for a UK entity offering services to EU data subjects. And the programme extending the architecture cannot be published until it is protected. SOC 2 and ISO 27001 are deliberately not in this round: they are procurement gates for a company with customers, and this one does not have customers yet.',
		milestone: 'Delaware C-corporation incorporated and able to receive institutional capital, EU representation appointed, priority filings made.',
		removes: 'Structural risk',
	},
	{
		n: '05',
		title: 'Operating',
		amount: '~$200K',
		pct: 13,
		unit: '18 months of everything else, including the relocation',
		body: 'Insurance, tooling, accounting, legal outside the filings above, and the San Francisco relocation. Listed rather than folded into another line, because a use-of-funds with no operating line has simply hidden it somewhere else.',
		milestone: '18 months of runway with every milestone above met inside it.',
		removes: 'Runway risk',
	},
	{
		n: '06',
		title: 'Buffer',
		amount: '~$150K',
		pct: 10,
		unit: 'Ten percent, unallocated and named as such',
		body: 'Held against the thing that has not been thought of. A scaling study that returns an unexpected result costs a third campaign; a dataset partner whose legal process runs six months longer than planned costs runway. Naming the buffer is the alternative to padding every line above it by ten percent and calling the total a budget.',
		milestone: 'Unspent, or spent against a decision written down at the time.',
		removes: 'Estimation risk',
	},
];

// Capital discipline reads louder than ambition at pre-seed, and what a founder refuses to spend
// on says more than what they will.
const notSpending = [
	{ k: 'Standing GPU capacity', v: 'The compute is a bounded campaign with a release date. Idle accelerators are the most common way a deep tech round disappears without a result attached.' },
	{ k: 'Sales headcount', v: 'There is nothing to scale yet. The first conversations are had by the person who built the architecture, because at this stage they are technical conversations.' },
	{ k: 'Marketing spend', v: 'The buyers are a few hundred institutions worldwide and they are reachable by name. Demand generation is not the constraint; evidence is.' },
	{ k: 'Exclusive data deals', v: 'Exclusive refreshed regulated data runs $500K–$5M a year and would consume the round several times over. One non-exclusive corpus proves the property that matters.' },
	{ k: 'Certification before customers', v: 'SOC 2 Type II and ISO 27001 are procurement gates for a company with a pipeline. Buying them now would be spending a fifth of the round to pass an exam nobody has set yet.' },
];

const derisked = [
	{ k: 'Does the mechanism work', v: 'Removed. 383 controlled experiments, 67 archived runs reproducible from seed, +70.3 pp recovery under structural collapse, published and checkable.', done: true },
	{ k: 'Is it a real architecture or a wrapper', v: 'Removed. 16,316 lines across 85 files, four architecture families, an adversarial suite the first version failed and the second had to clear.', done: true },
	{ k: 'Is the demand real', v: 'Removed by legislation rather than by us. The obligations arrive on dates already in law.', done: true },
	{ k: 'Does it hold beyond CIFAR', v: 'Live. This is what the compute in this round buys, on a transformer backbone, and the weak form of it is specified as one of four measurements.', done: false },
	{ k: 'Does it hold at frontier scale', v: 'Open, and not what this round claims to answer. That is the result the round after this one is priced against.', done: false },
	{ k: 'Does it hold on regulated data', v: 'Live. Requires one dataset partner, which is the third line item in this round.', done: false },
	{ k: 'Will institutions buy it', v: 'Live. Zero customer conversations have taken place.', done: false },
];

const risks = [
	{ q: 'No revenue, no customers, no pilot.', a: 'Zero customer conversations have taken place. Nothing on this site claims otherwise, and any diligence process will confirm it quickly. The reason is a date rather than a reluctance: the architecture reached maturity on 1 August 2026, and before that there was no product an institution could have been sold. Starting those conversations earlier would have meant selling a system that had not yet cleared its own destruction suite.' },
	{ q: 'One adverse published result.', a: 'Under adversarial weight injection the architecture is 1.0 pp worse than a parameter-matched baseline. It is published on the technology page and named in every market where it bears on a decision.' },
	{ q: 'Key-person risk is total.', a: 'One person wrote all of it. It is the first line item in the round and the largest, at roughly 43 percent, and it buys two people rather than five because three who can each work unsupervised is a company.' },
	{ q: 'The mechanism is being given away.', a: 'Deliberately, and on release rather than now. A result nobody can check is worth nothing to a regulator, and the defensibility was never intended to rest on secrecy. What is not published is the programme extending it, which stays unpublished until it is protected.' },
	{ q: 'The evidence base is CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100.', a: 'Stated on every page where a result appears. The architecture is domain-independent by construction; that is an argument, and this round converts it into a result.' },
	{ q: 'Regulatory timelines can move.', a: 'They can, and they have moved before: the Omnibus shifted Annex III to December 2027. They move later, not away. Every reset extends the window rather than closing it.' },
];

// ONE TRAIL, TWO CONSUMERS: the banner a reader sees and the BreadcrumbList a crawler
// reads. They used to be declared separately, in two shapes, and could not be kept in step —
// see the `trail` prop in layouts/Page.astro for what that cost.
const trail = [{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Invest' }];

---

<Page title="Pre-seed: the round and the risks" description={"Pre-seed, $1.5M, 18 months, six named milestones. Every regulated training run will soon have to account for itself, and almost none of them can."} trail={trail}>
	<PageBanner
		eyebrow="INVEST"
		title="Every model in a regulated sector will soon have to account for itself. Almost none of them can."
		trail={trail}
	/>

	<section class="section-tight container reveal">
		<div class="iv-brief">
			<p class="iv-brief-label">The whole thing, in one screen</p>
			<dl class="iv-brief-list">
				{brief.map((b) => (
					<div class="iv-brief-row">
						<dt>{b.k}</dt>
						<dd>{b.v}</dd>
					</div>
				))}
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container">
		<div class="iv-thesis">
			<p class="iv-thesis-lead">
				The entire field treats attribution as something reconstructed after training. It is not.
				It is something a network either computes about itself, or does not.
			</p>
			<div class="iv-thesis-body">
				<p class="iv-thesis-p">
					Interpretability, monitoring and governance tooling all share one assumption: that a
					simpler model of a trained network can be built and its approximation reported.
					That assumption is why two of those tools disagree on the same input, and why none of
					their output can be entered as evidence by an institution that is legally obliged to
					produce some.
				</p>
				<p class="iv-thesis-p">
					ORMAS bounds each node’s local gradient chain to four operations through a shared
					4,416-parameter bottleneck. Because the chain is bounded, per-node attribution stops
					being an estimate and becomes a measurement taken from the backward pass. A model
					trained this way arrives with the account already made.
				</p>
				<p class="iv-thesis-turn">
					If that is right, every regulated training run eventually has to be built this way, and
					the layer everyone is currently investing in is the wrong layer.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band--dark section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="iv-stats">
				{stats.map((s) => (
					<div class="iv-stat">
						<p class="iv-stat-figure tabular">
							{s.figure}{s.unit && <span class="iv-stat-unit">{s.unit}</span>}
						</p>
						<p class="iv-stat-label">{s.label}</p>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>WHY NOW</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="iv-h2" id="iv-timeline-h">The demand event has a date on it.</h2>
		<p class="iv-lede">
			The usual answer to “why has nobody built this” is that the incentive did not exist. Here it
			is arriving on a legislated calendar, and an architecture cannot be retrofitted into a model
			that has already been trained, so it has to exist before the date, not after it.
		</p>

		<figure class="iv-chart" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="iv-timeline-h">
			<svg
				viewBox={`0 0 ${CH.w} ${CH.h}`}
				class="iv-chart-svg"
				role="img"
				aria-label="Regulatory calendar from 2026 to 2031. SR 26-2 effective April 2026. EU AI Act Omnibus in force July 2026. EU AI Act Annex III high-risk obligations apply 2 December 2027. Annex I obligations apply 2 August 2028. European Health Data Space first phase 2029, full scope 2031."
			>
				{years.map((y) => (
					<g>
						<line
							x1={x(`${y}-01-01`)} y1={CH.top - 22}
							x2={x(`${y}-01-01`)} y2={CH.h - 20}
							class="iv-chart-grid"
						/>
						<text x={x(`${y}-01-01`)} y={CH.top - 30} class="iv-chart-year">{y}</text>
					</g>
				))}

				<line
					x1={x(today)} y1={CH.top - 22}
					x2={x(today)} y2={CH.h - 20}
					class="iv-chart-now"
				/>
				<text x={x(today)} y={CH.h - 6} class="iv-chart-nowlabel">TODAY</text>

				{events.map((e, i) => {
					const cy = CH.top + i * CH.rowH;
					return (
						<g>
							<text x={CH.labelW - 16} y={cy - 2} class="iv-chart-label">{e.label}</text>
							<text x={CH.labelW - 16} y={cy + 13} class="iv-chart-who">{e.who}</text>
							<line x1={CH.labelW} y1={cy + 1} x2={x(e.date) - 7} y2={cy + 1} class="iv-chart-lead" />
							<circle
								cx={x(e.date)} cy={cy + 1} r="5.5"
								class={e.past ? 'iv-chart-dot iv-chart-dot--past' : 'iv-chart-dot'}
							/>
						</g>
					);
				})}
			</svg>
			<figcaption class="iv-chart-cap">
				Dates in law as at September 2026. Filled marks have already taken effect.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<p class="iv-note">
			Two of these have already happened. An institution that begins the architecture question in
			2027 is not late by a quarter; it is late by a training cycle, and a training cycle in a
			regulated environment is measured in years.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>THE MARKET</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="iv-h2">Counted upward, from the institutions that qualify.</h2>
		<p class="iv-lede">
			The reference market is AI model-risk management: $8.3B in 2026, reaching $19.1B by 2035 on
			a 9.7% compound rate. That budget line already exists inside every institution below. Today
			it buys tools that estimate a finished model from the outside.
		</p>
		<p class="iv-note">
			An institution qualifies for this architecture on three tests at once. It holds data that
			cannot legally leave its infrastructure. It has an active plan to train on that data. And it
			answers to somebody who will ask what the model learned. Roughly a thousand clear all three.
		</p>
		<div class="iv-bench">
